  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1151 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nTonight \n\nThe 27.12z models are in general agreement that a shortwave trough \nwill move east through Upper and Mid-Mississippi River valleys east \ninto the Great Lakes tonight. Like yesterday, there is concern \non the low level saturation. While there is currently good radar\nreturns over western Minnesota and eastern South Dakota, just \nmainly seeing traces along the surface front. Due to this, kept \nthe precipitation probabilities below 24 percent for tonight.\n\nAnother concern tonight is the precipitation type along and north of \nInterstate 94. Soundings look to be a bit colder than yesterday, so \nif there is any melting below 5K feet, it would be only partial. As \na result, removed the freezing rain/drizzle and just went with rain \nand snow. Like the past several days, precipitation amounts look to \nbe on the light side (ranging from a trace to maybe a hundredth).\n\nLow temperatures tonight will range from the mid-20s to lower 30s.\n\nSaturday\n\nWesterly winds will bring Pacific air into the region. As a result, \nhigh temperatures will be warmer than they will be this afternoon. \nHigh temperatures will be in the lower and mid-40s north of \nInterstate 94 and mid- to upper 40s elsewhere.\n\nSaturday Night and Sunday\n\nThere continues to be some phasing of the northern and southern \nstreams. With the stronger wave being in the southern stream and \nthat wave digging further south, the track of the low is trending \nfurther and further south. Due to this, took out the precipitation \nchances along and north of Interstate 90 and held the highest (40-70 \npercent) precipitation chances in our far southern counties. \nSoundings continue to indicate that the precipitation type will\nbe mainly rain. However, if the precipitation lingers into the \nmorning, it might be cold enough for some light snow to mix in \nprior to the precipitation ending.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-20s to mid-30s on\nSaturday night. High temperatures will be in the 30s on Sunday.\n\nMonday through Thursday\n\nOn Monday and Tuesday, the 500 mb pattern will be mainly zonal \nacross the northern US and southern Canada. As a result, \ntemperatures will be near to slightly above normal (in the 30s).\nOn Tuesday night, a trough will move into the southwest US and \nthen it moves east into the Southern and Central Plains on \nWednesday night and Thursday. The winds aloft will become more \nsoutherly. High temperatures will range from the mid-40s to \nmid-50s. These temperatures are more typical of late October and\nearly November.\n\nThursday night into Friday\n\nThere is much uncertainty on where the low pressure will track after \nit leaves the Central and Southern Plains. Some models phase the \nnorthern and southern streams and this results in rapid deepening of \na surface low which will be tracking northeast toward the Great \nLakes. Other models keep suggest little or no phasing between \nthe two streams and this results in a weaker surface low and \nmore southern track. \n\nLow temperatures will range from the lower 30s to lower 40s on \nThursday night. High temperatures will range from the lower 40s to \nlower 50s.",
